   347	### Honest caveat
   348	
   349	If the bottleneck we care about is the **compile-time StatevectorSqueezePass /
   350	tier classification / MLIR emission** (host-side codegen decisions) rather than
   351	the runtime amdgcn kernel body, none of these loops help — they optimize a fixed
   352	kernel body against a fixed driver. They fit best if the target is the *runtime
   353	interpreter kernel* (VGPR/LDS/occupancy, the AGPR-traffic flag in
   354	`TOOLING_STATUS.md`). Given LDS (24.5 KB) is already flagged as the occupancy
   355	limiter and VGPR is low (64/256), there IS a runtime-kernel optimization surface
   356	worth pointing a loop at — so KernelForge is a reasonable investment. Start with
   357	the no-agent smoke test (step 4): if the driver + bench parse cleanly, the
   358	LLM-in-the-loop cost is the only remaining question.

